How old GUI fires an attribute (read this first; every row leans on it)
    Bwana.twk registerMethods (129-198) maps an attribute NAME to a C++
    method, in one of three ways, and the way IS the moment:
        mapMethod     layout   runs during layout (layoutAction), each layout
        mapImmediate  layout   runs the first time layout meets it, then never
        mapAction     event    deferred, run on user input (deferredAction);
                               the named actions are in Actions.twk 424-436
                               (card display expandTree fire iMAGEwork menu
                               reBigify sORT tab toggle)
    Most layout methods do their real work by calling setAction(item,"name"),
    which hangs one of those event actions on the field. setAction itself is
    NOT FOUND -- not in Groups/GUI, Bot, data/support, Include/GUIexternals or
    groups.ext; old GUI calls it as ::setAction (Bwana.mm:43).
    Events (Layout.twk fireAction 160-218) BUBBLE: starting at the clicked
    block, it runs the block's own method, then every attribute carrying a
    deferred method, matching left/right click and key events, then moves to
    the parent until something has fired.
    Order matters and is only partly handled: panel, drawPath, window first;
    describe and show last (show is "modified", so Details processMethods
    runs it late). Bwana's comment says listing order does not guarantee it.
    Inheritance: tok `x%name` is getAttribute (own attributes only),
    `x[name]` is get (own child of any kind); only `findAttribute` walks the
    parents (GroupItem.mm:981). So an attribute is inherited only where its
    reader says findAttribute.
    The per-field layout state is a Details object (Details.twk), reached by
    item.getDetail(); every row's "detail" flag lives there.

Row fields: did / where / when / inherited / leans on / C++ floor / lean.

