Bot/PDF.twk (523 lines, 2021-03-31; its header calls it "a test class to
experiment with printing a layout to pdf")
    does:       paints an already laid-out form tree to one PDF page. One walk
                (drawPDFdetail, walkVisible) over the visible fields; per field
                it saves graphics state, sets line width / fill / stroke,
                draws the frame (setFrame: rounded box, rectangle, or the
                per-side top/bottom/left/right borders), then paints the
                content by kind -- cell or text (displayCell), image
                (displayImage, jpg/png, scaled to fill and cropped, offset and
                scale attributes), path (displayPath, the drawing language
                through DrawPoint.drawPDFpath, with clip-then-image).
                Output: drawPDF writes <tag>.pdf (pdfDirectory); Bot's
                Layout.drawRect instead saves to a memory stream
                (HPDF_SaveToStream, HPDF_GetStreamSize in Layout.mm) and
                shows it in a PDFView -- the window IS a PDF.
    draws by:   neither Core Graphics nor hand-built PDF -- the LIBHARU C
                library (HPDF_*), wrapped by support/Include/pdf.ext as tok
                aliases (PDFdocument, PDFpage, writeInFrame, pdfArcTo ...).
                ⚠ The library is not on this machine: TOK.xcodeproj points its
                headers at /Volumes/IOmega/optLocal/include/hpdf.h (an external
                drive), and no libhpdf was found. Bot is not in the build.
    primitives: document/page (HPDF_New, AddPage, SetWidth/Height, SaveToFile,
                SaveToStream); path (MoveTo, LineTo, CurveTo, Arc, Rectangle,
                ClosePath, Clip); paint (Fill, Stroke, FillStroke,
                SetRGBFill/Stroke, SetLineWidth, GSave/GRestore); image
                (LoadJpeg/PngImageFromFile, DrawImage, GetWidth/Height); text
                (BeginText/EndText, SetFontAndSize, TextRect); fonts
                (LoadTTFontFromFile, LoadTTCFontFromFile, GetFont, GetAscent,
                GetDescent). That list is the C++ floor almost exactly as the
                typesetting notes name it: measure text, draw glyphs, draw
                lines, plus images.
    text, fonts: a fONTs entry names a directory and a file; ttf or ttc is
                loaded from disk (ttc: walks faces for "Bold" / "Italic") and
                the loaded font is cached on the font entry as a pointer
                attribute (pdfFont, bold, italic). Size comes from fontSize
                (findAttribute, so inherited) and is required. Line height =
                size x (ascent - descent) / 1000. Colours parse through the
                RGBvalue rule. Measuring: textWidth (HPDF_Page_MeasureText) is
                declared in pdf.ext and NEVER CALLED -- kant itself measures
                nothing.
    breaks:     lines, yes, but not itself: writeInFrame is HPDF_Page_TextRect,
                which word-wraps greedily inside one rectangle (left / center
                / right / justify) and returns how many characters it printed.
                That count (charactersPrinted) is THROWN AWAY -- text that does
                not fit is lost, never carried to a next frame. Pages: no. One
                page, sized to the window (pageWidth = layout width).
    note:       PDF's origin is bottom-left, so convertOrigin flips every
                field's frame in the tree before drawing and flips it back
                after -- the paint walk mutates the tree it walks, twice.
    lean:       BORROW IDEAS, LEAVE THE CODE. libharu is a dependency this
                machine no longer has, and the Display ruling already chose
                Core Text as the measurer -- Core Graphics writes PDF itself
                (CGPDFContext), so it covers libharu's job on the platform
                already chosen. Worth borrowing: one paint walk dispatching by
                content kind; per-side borders; the rounded-box path; the
                font cache on the font entry; clip-then-image; and above all
                TextRect's shape -- "fill this frame, tell me how much you
                used" -- which is the pour primitive the typesetting notes
                need, with the returned count kept as the next frame's start
                instead of discarded. Not to borrow: the in-place origin flip
                (flip at the emit boundary instead) and greedy line breaking.

